%% Part II: Programming Language Design

%% ============================================================
%% 14.1 Introduction
%% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:paradigms-intro}

A \emph{programming paradigm} is a fundamental style or approach to
programming that defines how computation is structured and how programmers
think about the problems they are solving.  Paradigms are not merely
collections of language features; they are \emph{ways of thinking} about
computation.  Each paradigm provides a different lens through which to view
problems, and different problems are illuminated by different lenses.

In this chapter, we examine the major programming paradigms, their
theoretical foundations, their influence on language design, and the ways in
which modern languages combine multiple paradigms.  We also explore how
paradigm choices affect compiler and interpreter design.

\begin{definition}[Programming Paradigm]\label{def:paradigm}
  A \emph{programming paradigm} is a coherent approach to programming
  characterized by:
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item A model of computation (what is being computed).
    \item A style of program organization (how programs are structured).
    \item A set of abstractions (the concepts available to the programmer).
    \item A discipline of reasoning (how correctness is established).
  \end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

The paradigms we cover are:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Imperative programming (\Cref{sec:imperative-paradigm})
  \item Functional programming (\Cref{sec:functional-paradigm})
  \item Object-oriented programming (\Cref{sec:oop-paradigm})
  \item Logic programming (\Cref{sec:logic-paradigm})
  \item Constraint programming (\Cref{sec:constraint-paradigm})
  \item Multi-paradigm languages (\Cref{sec:multi-paradigm})
  \item Data-driven and reactive programming (\Cref{sec:data-driven-reactive})
  \item Concurrent and distributed paradigms (\Cref{sec:concurrent-paradigm})
  \item Paradigm influence on compiler design (\Cref{sec:paradigm-compiler})
\end{itemize}

%% ============================================================
%% 14.2 Imperative Programming
%% ============================================================
\section{Imperative Programming}\label{sec:imperative-paradigm}

The imperative paradigm is the oldest and most intuitive: a program is a
sequence of commands that modify the program state.  Computation is
understood as a series of state transitions.

\subsection{The State-Transition Model}\label{sec:state-transition}

\begin{definition}[Imperative Computation]\label{def:imperative}
  \emph{Imperative computation} is modeled as a sequence of state
  transitions.  A \emph{state} is a mapping from variable names to values.
  A \emph{command} transforms one state into another.  A \emph{program} is
  a composition of commands.
\end{definition}

The fundamental semantic model of imperative programming is the
\emph{state transformer}:
\[
  \semS : \Stmt \to (\State \to \State)
\]
where $\Stmt$ is the set of statements and $\State = \Var \to \Val$ is the
set of states.

\subsection{Core Constructs}\label{sec:imperative-constructs}

The imperative paradigm is built on a small set of core constructs:

\begin{grammar}{Imperative Core}
  \production{S}{x \coloneqq E \alt \mathbf{skip} \alt S_1\, ;\, S_2} \\
  \altproduces \mathbf{if}\ B\ \mathbf{then}\ S_1\ \mathbf{else}\ S_2 \\
  \altproduces \mathbf{while}\ B\ \mathbf{do}\ S \\
  \altproduces \mathbf{for}\ x \coloneqq E_1\ \mathbf{to}\ E_2\ \mathbf{do}\ S
\end{grammar}

These constructs correspond to the three fundamental control structures
identified by B\"ohm and Jacopini~\cite{bohm1966diagrammi}:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Sequence}: $S_1 ; S_2$
  \item \textbf{Selection}: $\mathbf{if}\ B\ \mathbf{then}\ S_1\ \mathbf{else}\ S_2$
  \item \textbf{Iteration}: $\mathbf{while}\ B\ \mathbf{do}\ S$
\end{enumerate}

\begin{theorem}[B\"ohm--Jacopini]\label{thm:bohm-jacopini}
  Any computable function can be implemented using only sequence, selection,
  and iteration.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Formal Semantics of While}\label{sec:while-semantics}

The while language is the canonical imperative language for studying
semantics.  Its big-step semantics is:

\begin{semrules}{While Language Big-Step}
  \semruleaxiom{
    \bconfig{\mathbf{skip}, \sigma} \evalto \sigma
  }{\text{(Skip)}} \\[6pt]
  \semrule{
    \semB \sembrack{b}_\sigma = \mathbf{true} \quad
    \bconfig{S_1, \sigma} \evalto \sigma' \quad
    \bconfig{S_2, \sigma'} \evalto \sigma''
  }{
    \bconfig{S_1 ; S_2, \sigma} \evalto \sigma''
  }{\text{(Seq)}} \\[6pt]
  \semrule{
    \semB \sembrack{b}_\sigma = \mathbf{true} \quad
    \bconfig{S, \sigma} \evalto \sigma' \quad
    \bconfig{\mathbf{while}\ b\ \mathbf{do}\ S, \sigma'} \evalto \sigma''
  }{
    \bconfig{\mathbf{while}\ b\ \mathbf{do}\ S, \sigma} \evalto \sigma''
  }{\text{(While-T)}} \\[6pt]
  \semrule{
    \semB \sembrack{b}_\sigma = \mathbf{false}
  }{
    \bconfig{\mathbf{while}\ b\ \mathbf{do}\ S, \sigma} \evalto \sigma
  }{\text{(While-F)}}
\end{semrules}

\subsection{Aliasing and Side Effects}\label{sec:aliasing-side-effects}

The imperative paradigm introduces two concepts that complicate reasoning:

\begin{definition}[Alias]\label{def:alias}
  Two expressions are \emph{aliases} if they denote the same memory
  location.  Modifying one alias is visible through the other.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Side Effect]\label{def:side-effect}
  A \emph{side effect} is a modification of state that occurs as a result
  of evaluating an expression.  An expression with side effects is not
  referentially transparent.
\end{definition}

Aliasing and side effects are the primary obstacles to optimization and
verification of imperative programs.  The compiler must perform alias
analysis to determine when modifications through one pointer are visible
through another.

\begin{historical}
The structured programming movement, led by Edsger Dijkstra in the 1960s,
argued that the \texttt{goto} statement was harmful and should be replaced
by structured control flow (sequence, selection, iteration).  This was a
paradigm-defining moment: it established that the \emph{structure} of
programs matters, not just their input--output behavior.
\end{historical}

%% ============================================================
%% 14.3 Functional Programming
%% ============================================================
\section{Functional Programming}\label{sec:functional-paradigm}

The functional paradigm views computation as the evaluation of mathematical
functions.  Programs are composed of function definitions and applications,
and the fundamental operation is \emph{substitution} rather than
\emph{mutation}.

\subsection{The Lambda Calculus Foundation}\label{sec:lambda-foundation}

Functional programming is grounded in the lambda calculus
(\Cref{ch:untyped-lambda}, \Cref{ch:stlc}).  The core ideas are:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Functions as values}: Functions can be passed as arguments,
    returned as results, and stored in data structures.
  \item \textbf{Referential transparency}: Expressions can be replaced by
    their values without changing program behavior.
  \item \textbf{Immutability}: Data is not modified; new data is created
    from old.
  \item \textbf{Recursion}: Iteration is expressed through recursive
    function calls rather than loops.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Pure vs. Impure Functional Programming}\label{sec:pure-vs-impure}

\begin{definition}[Pure Functional Language]\label{def:pure-functional}
  A \emph{pure} functional language is one in which all expressions are
  referentially transparent.  There is no mutable state, no I/O as a side
  effect, and no exceptions.  Examples: Haskell, Clean.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Impure Functional Language]\label{def:impure-functional}
  An \emph{impure} functional language supports first-class functions and
  functional programming style but also allows mutable state and side
  effects.  Examples: ML, OCaml, F\#, Scheme, Lisp.
\end{definition}

The distinction matters for reasoning: in a pure language, the equation
$f(x) + f(x) = 2 \times f(x)$ always holds; in an impure language, it may
not (if $f$ has side effects).

\subsection{Key Abstractions}\label{sec:functional-abstractions}

Functional programming provides several powerful abstractions:

\subsubsection{Higher-Order Functions}

\begin{example}[Higher-Order Functions]\label{ex:higher-order}
  The classic higher-order functions \texttt{map}, \texttt{filter}, and
  \texttt{fold} capture common iteration patterns:
  \begin{align*}
    \texttt{map} & : (\alpha \to \beta) \to [\alpha] \to [\beta] \\
    \texttt{filter} & : (\alpha \to \Bool) \to [\alpha] \to [\alpha] \\
    \texttt{fold} & : (\alpha \to \beta \to \beta) \to \beta \to [\alpha] \to \beta
  \end{align*}
\end{example}

\subsubsection{Pattern Matching}

Pattern matching allows functions to be defined by cases, decomposing data
structures and binding variables simultaneously:

\begin{semrules}{Pattern Matching}
  \semrule{
    \texttt{match}\ v\ \texttt{with}\ p_i \to e_i \quad
    v \text{ matches } p_k \text{ with substitution } \theta
  }{
    \texttt{match}\ v\ \texttt{with}\ p_i \to e_i \evalto \theta(e_k)
  }{\text{(Match)}}
\end{semrules}

\subsubsection{Monads and Effects}

Pure functional languages handle effects (I/O, state, exceptions) through
\emph{monads} (\Cref{ch:monads}).  A monad encapsulates effectful
computations while maintaining referential transparency.

\begin{definition}[Monad]\label{def:monad-paradigm}
  A \emph{monad} is a triple $(M, \eta, \bind)$ where $M$ is a type
  constructor, $\eta : \alpha \to M\,\alpha$ is \emph{return}, and
  $\bind : M\,\alpha \to (\alpha \to M\,\beta) \to M\,\beta$ is
  \emph{bind}, satisfying the monad laws:
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item $\eta\, a \bind k = k\, a$ (left unit)
    \item $m \bind \eta = m$ (right unit)
    \item $(m \bind k) \bind h = m \bind (\lambda x.\, k\, x \bind h)$ (associativity)
  \end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Lazy vs. Eager Evaluation}\label{sec:lazy-vs-eager}

\begin{definition}[Evaluation Strategy]\label{def:eval-strategy}
  An \emph{eager} (strict) evaluation strategy evaluates function arguments
  before applying the function.  A \emph{lazy} (non-strict) evaluation
  strategy delays evaluation of arguments until their values are needed.
\end{definition}

Lazy evaluation enables:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Infinite data structures}: Lists, trees, and other structures
    can be conceptually infinite.
  \item \textbf{Compositional programming}: Programs can be written as
    compositions of generators and consumers without concern for efficiency.
  \item \textbf{Modularity}: Separation of ``what to compute'' from ``when
    to compute it.''
\end{itemize}

\begin{historical}
The debate between lazy and eager evaluation has a long history.  Landin's
SECD machine (1964) introduced lazy evaluation.  The ISWIM language
(Landin, 1966) was lazy.  But most practical languages chose eager evaluation
for predictability and efficiency.  Haskell's success demonstrated that lazy
evaluation can be practical.
\end{historical}

%% ============================================================
%% 14.4 Object-Oriented Programming
%% ============================================================
\section{Object-Oriented Programming}\label{sec:oop-paradigm}

The object-oriented paradigm organizes programs around \emph{objects}---
entities that combine data and behavior, communicating through message
passing.

\subsection{Core Concepts}\label{sec:oop-concepts}

\begin{definition}[Object]\label{def:object}
  An \emph{object} is a computational entity that encapsulates state
  (fields, attributes) and behavior (methods).  Objects interact by sending
  messages (method calls) to each other.
\end{definition}

The three pillars of object-oriented programming are:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Encapsulation}: The internal state of an object is hidden;
    access is through a well-defined interface.
  \item \textbf{Inheritance}: Classes can inherit state and behavior from
    parent classes, enabling code reuse and hierarchical organization.
  \item \textbf{Polymorphism}: Objects of different classes can respond to
    the same message in different ways (dynamic dispatch).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Class-Based vs. Prototype-Based OOP}\label{sec:class-vs-prototype}

There are two fundamentally different approaches to object-oriented
programming:

\begin{definition}[Class-Based OOP]\label{def:class-based}
  In \emph{class-based} OOP, objects are instances of classes.  Classes
  define the structure and behavior of their instances.  Inheritance is
  between classes.  Examples: Java, C++, C\#, Ruby.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Prototype-Based OOP]\label{def:prototype-based}
  In \emph{prototype-based} OOP, objects are cloned from other objects
  (prototypes).  There are no classes; inheritance is delegation to a
  prototype.  Examples: Self, JavaScript, Io.
\end{definition}

\begin{insight}
Prototype-based OOP is more fundamental than class-based OOP.  Classes can
be simulated using prototypes (a class is just a prototype with a
constructor), but prototypes cannot be fully captured by classes.  The
prototype model is closer to the original vision of OOP as expressed in
Smalltalk.
\end{insight}

\subsection{Method Dispatch}\label{sec:method-dispatch}

The semantics of method dispatch is central to OOP:

\begin{semrules}{Dynamic Dispatch}
  \semrule{
    \Obj{o}.\texttt{class} = C \quad
    C.\texttt{methods}(m) = \lambda \texttt{this}.\, e \quad
    e[\Obj{o}/\texttt{this}] \Downarrow v
  }{
    \Obj{o}.m(\vec{v}) \Downarrow v
  }{\text{(Dispatch)}}
\end{semrules}

\emph{Single dispatch} selects the method based on the runtime type of the
receiver.  \emph{Multiple dispatch} (as in CLOS) selects based on the types
of all arguments.

\subsection{The Subtyping Relationship}\label{sec:subtyping-oop}

OOP introduces \emph{subtyping}: if $C$ is a subclass of $D$, then $C$ is a
subtype of $D$, and objects of type $C$ can be used wherever objects of type
$D$ are expected.

\begin{definition}[Subtype Polymorphism]\label{def:subtype-poly}
  If $C <: D$ (read ``$C$ is a subtype of $D$''), then any expression of
  type $C$ can be used in a context expecting type $D$.  This is the
  \emph{substitution principle} (Liskov substitution).
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Liskov Substitution Principle]\label{thm:liskov}
  If $S$ is a subtype of $T$, then objects of type $T$ can be replaced with
  objects of type $S$ without altering any desirable properties of the
  program (correctness, postconditions, invariants).
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Object Calculus}\label{sec:object-calculus}

The formal foundations of OOP have been studied through object calculi.
The most influential is the $\varsigma$-calculus of Abadi and
Cardelli~\cite{abadi1996theory}:

\begin{grammar}{Object Calculus}
  \production{a}{x \alt [l_i = \varsigma(x_i)\, b_i^{i \in 1..n}] \alt a.l \alt a.l \coloneqq b \alt \mathbf{let}\ x = a\ \mathbf{in}\ b}
\end{grammar}

An object $[l_i = \varsigma(x_i)\, b_i^{i \in 1..n}]$ is a collection of
methods $l_i$, each defined as a function of the self-parameter $x_i$ and
the body $b_i$.

%% ============================================================
%% 14.5 Logic Programming
%% ============================================================
\section{Logic Programming}\label{sec:logic-paradigm}

The logic programming paradigm views computation as \emph{deductive
inference}.  A program is a set of logical assertions, and computation is
the process of proving theorems from these assertions.

\subsection{The Relational Model}\label{sec:relational-model}

\begin{definition}[Logic Program]\label{def:logic-program}
  A \emph{logic program} is a set of \emph{clauses} (Horn clauses) of the
  form:
  \[
    A \texttt{:-} B_1, B_2, \ldots, B_n
  \]
  where $A$ is the \emph{head} (an atom) and $B_1, \ldots, B_n$ is the
  \emph{body} (a conjunction of atoms).  The clause is read: ``$A$ is true
  if $B_1$ and $B_2$ and \ldots and $B_n$ are true.''
\end{definition}

\subsection{Prolog: The Archetypal Logic Language}\label{sec:prolog}

\begin{historical}
Prolog was created by Alain Colmerauer and Philippe Roussel at the
University of Marseille in 1972, based on work by Robert Kowalski on the
executable subset of first-order logic~\cite{colmerauer1973prolog}.  It
became the dominant logic programming language and was widely used in
artificial intelligence.
\end{historical}

A Prolog program consists of \emph{facts} and \emph{rules}:

\begin{example}[Prolog Program]\label{ex:prolog}
  \begin{verbatim}
  % Facts
  parent(tom, bob).
  parent(tom, liz).
  parent(bob, ann).
  
  % Rules
  grandparent(X, Z) :- parent(X, Y), parent(Y, Z).
  ancestor(X, Y) :- parent(X, Y).
  ancestor(X, Y) :- parent(X, Z), ancestor(Z, Y).
  \end{verbatim}
  
  Query: \texttt{?- grandparent(tom, ann).} returns \texttt{true}.
\end{example}

\subsection{Unification and Resolution}\label{sec:unification-resolution}

The computational mechanisms of logic programming are \emph{unification} and
\emph{resolution}:

\begin{definition}[Unification]\label{def:unification}
  \emph{Unification} is the process of finding a substitution $\theta$ that
  makes two terms identical: $t_1\theta = t_2\theta$.  The most general
  unifier (mgu) is the substitution that makes the terms identical with the
  least commitment.
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
  \caption{Unification Algorithm}
  \label{alg:unification}
  \begin{algorithmic}[1]
    \Function{Unify}{$t_1, t_2$}
      \If{$t_1 = t_2$}
        \State \Return $\{\}$ \Comment{empty substitution}
      \ElsIf{$t_1$ is a variable $x$}
        \If{$x$ occurs in $t_2$}
          \State \Return \textbf{fail} \Comment{occurs check}
        \Else
          \State \Return $\{x \mapsto t_2\}$
        \EndIf
      \ElsIf{$t_2$ is a variable $x$}
        \State \Return \Call{Unify}{$t_2, t_1$}
      \ElsIf{$t_1 = f(s_1, \ldots, s_n)$ and $t_2 = f(t_1', \ldots, t_n')$}
        \State $\theta \gets \{\}$
        \For{$i = 1$ to $n$}
          \State $\theta_i \gets \Call{Unify}{s_i\theta, t_i'\theta}$
          \If{$\theta_i = \textbf{fail}$}
            \State \Return \textbf{fail}
          \EndIf
          \State $\theta \gets \theta \circ \theta_i$
        \EndFor
        \State \Return $\theta$
      \Else
        \State \Return \textbf{fail}
      \EndIf
    \EndFunction
  \end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Search Strategies}\label{sec:logic-search}

Logic programming languages differ in their search strategies:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Depth-first search (Prolog)}: Explores one branch
    completely before backtracking.  Efficient but incomplete (may loop on
    left-recursive rules).
  \item \textbf{Breadth-first search}: Explores all branches level by level.
    Complete but memory-intensive.
  \item \textbf{Best-first search}: Uses heuristics to guide search.
  \item \textbf{Constraint logic programming}: Combines logic programming
    with constraint solving (\Cref{sec:constraint-paradigm}).
\end{itemize}

%% ============================================================
%% 14.6 Constraint Programming
%% ============================================================
\section{Constraint Programming}\label{sec:constraint-paradigm}

The constraint programming paradigm views computation as the satisfaction of
a set of constraints.  The programmer declares what must be true; the
solver determines how to make it true.

\subsection{Constraint Satisfaction Problems}\label{sec:csp}

\begin{definition}[CSP]\label{def:csp}
  A \emph{constraint satisfaction problem} (CSP) is a triple $(X, D, C)$
  where:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item $X = \{x_1, \ldots, x_n\}$ is a set of variables.
    \item $D = \{D_1, \ldots, D_n\}$ is a set of domains, where $D_i$ is
      the set of values variable $x_i$ can take.
    \item $C = \{c_1, \ldots, c_m\}$ is a set of constraints, where each
      $c_j$ restricts the values that a subset of variables can take
      simultaneously.
  \end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Constraint Solving}\label{sec:constraint-solving}

Constraint solvers use a combination of techniques:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Domain propagation}: Narrow variable domains based on
    constraints.
  \item \textbf{Constraint propagation}: Deduce new constraints from
    existing ones.
  \item \textbf{Backtracking search}: Systematically try values and
    backtrack on failure.
  \item \textbf{Local search}: Heuristic methods for large problems.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Constraint Logic Programming}\label{sec:clp}

Constraint logic programming (CLP) combines logic programming with
constraint solving.  Languages in this family include CLP(R) (real numbers),
CLP(FD) (finite domains), and Eclipse.

\begin{example}[CLP Example]\label{ex:clp}
  The $n$-queens problem in CLP(FD):
  \begin{verbatim}
  n_queens(N, Qs) :-
    length(Qs, N),
    Qs ins 1..N,
    all_different(Qs),
    safe_queens(Qs),
    label(Qs).
  \end{verbatim}
  The constraint solver handles domain constraints and
  \texttt{all\_different}; the programmer only declares the problem.
\end{example}

%% ============================================================
%% 14.7 Multi-Paradigm Languages
%% ============================================================
\section{Multi-Paradigm Languages}\label{sec:multi-paradigm}

Modern programming languages increasingly support multiple paradigms,
allowing programmers to choose the most appropriate style for each part of
their program.

\subsection{The Case for Multi-Paradigm}\label{sec:case-multi}

\begin{insight}
No single paradigm is best for all problems.  A multi-paradigm language
allows programmers to use the right tool for each job: functional style for
data transformation, object-oriented style for modular design, imperative
style for performance-critical code, and logic/constraint style for
combinatorial problems.
\end{insight}

\subsection{Examples of Multi-Paradigm Languages}\label{sec:multi-examples}

\begin{table}[htbp]
  \centering
  \caption{Multi-Paradigm Language Features}
  \label{tab:multi-paradigm}
  \begin{tabular}{lcccc}
    \hline
    \textbf{Language} & \textbf{Imperative} & \textbf{Functional} & \textbf{OOP} & \textbf{Other} \\
    \hline
    Scala & \checkmark & \checkmark & \checkmark & Actor model \\
    F\# & \checkmark & \checkmark & \checkmark & Computation exprs \\
    Rust & \checkmark & \checkmark & Partial & Ownership \\
    Kotlin & \checkmark & \checkmark & \checkmark & Coroutines \\
    Swift & \checkmark & \checkmark & \checkmark & Protocol-oriented \\
    OCaml & \checkmark & \checkmark & \checkmark & Modules \\
    Python & \checkmark & Partial & \checkmark & Generators \\
    C++ & \checkmark & Partial & \checkmark & Templates, concepts \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Design Challenges}\label{sec:multi-challenges}

Multi-paradigm languages face several design challenges:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Coherence}: Do the paradigms integrate smoothly, or do they
    feel like separate languages bolted together?
  \item \textbf{Complexity}: Supporting multiple paradigms increases the
    language's complexity, making it harder to learn and implement.
  \item \textbf{Performance}: Some paradigm combinations have performance
    implications (e.g., garbage collection for functional style in a
    systems language).
  \item \textbf{Tooling}: IDE support, debuggers, and profilers must
    understand all paradigms.
\end{enumerate}

%% ============================================================
%% 14.8 Data-Driven and Reactive Programming
%% ============================================================
\section{Data-Driven and Reactive Programming}\label{sec:data-driven-reactive}

\subsection{Data-Driven Programming}\label{sec:data-driven}

\begin{definition}[Data-Driven Programming]\label{def:data-driven}
  In \emph{data-driven programming}, the flow of computation is determined
  by the availability of data rather than by explicit control flow.  A
  computation is triggered when its input data becomes available.
\end{definition}

Data-driven programming is the foundation of:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Dataflow languages}: Lucid, Id, Sisal---where computation
    proceeds when data arrives at nodes in a dataflow graph.
  \item \textbf{Spreadsheet computation}: Cells are recomputed when their
    dependencies change.
  \item \textbf{Build systems}: Make, Bazel, Ninja---targets are rebuilt
    when their dependencies change.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Reactive Programming}\label{sec:reactive}

\begin{definition}[Reactive Programming]\label{def:reactive}
  \emph{Reactive programming} is a paradigm where programs are defined in
  terms of data streams and the propagation of change.  Values are not
  static; they change over time, and dependent values are automatically
  updated.
\end{definition}

Reactive programming manifests in several forms:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Functional reactive programming (FRP)}: Time-varying values
    (behaviors) and event streams are first-class.  Examples: Elm,
    React (inspired by), Yampa.
  \item \textbf{Observable streams}: Libraries like RxJS, RxJava, and
    Combine provide operators for transforming and combining streams.
  \item \textbf{Spreadsheet-like reactivity}: Frameworks like Vue.js and
    Svelte automatically update the UI when data changes.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}[FRP Behavior]\label{ex:frp}
  In FRP, a \emph{behavior} is a time-varying value:
  \begin{align*}
    \texttt{position} & : \texttt{Behavior Point} \\
    \texttt{velocity} & : \texttt{Behavior Vector} \\
    \texttt{position} & = \texttt{integral}(\texttt{velocity})
  \end{align*}
  The position is always the integral of the velocity; when velocity
  changes, position is automatically updated.
\end{example}

%% ============================================================
%% 14.9 Concurrent and Distributed Paradigms
%% ============================================================
\section{Concurrent and Distributed Paradigms}\label{sec:concurrent-paradigm}

\subsection{The Actor Model}\label{sec:actor-model}

\begin{definition}[Actor]\label{def:actor}
  An \emph{actor} is a computational entity that:
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item Can send messages to other actors.
    \item Can create new actors.
    \item Can designate what to do with the next message received.
  \end{enumerate}
  Actors execute concurrently and communicate only through asynchronous
  message passing.
\end{definition}

The actor model, introduced by Hewitt, Bishop, and Steiger~\cite{hewitt1973universal},
provides a foundation for concurrent and distributed computation.  Languages
based on the actor model include Erlang, Akka (Scala), and Pony.

\subsection{The Pi-Calculus}\label{sec:pi-calculus}

The $\pi$-calculus of Milner~\cite{milner1992calculus} is a formal model of
concurrent computation based on communication along channels:

\begin{grammar}{Pi-Calculus}
  \production{P}{\mathbf{0} \alt \bar{x}\langle y \rangle.P \alt x(y).P \alt P_1 \mid P_2 \alt \mathbf{!}P \alt (\nu\, x)P}
\end{grammar}

\begin{itemize}
  \item $\bar{x}\langle y \rangle.P$: Send $y$ on channel $x$, then continue as $P$.
  \item $x(y).P$: Receive a value on channel $x$, bind it to $y$, continue as $P$.
  \item $P_1 \mid P_2$: Parallel composition.
  \item $(\nu\, x)P$: Restrict channel $x$ to scope $P$ (private channel).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Software Transactional Memory}\label{sec:stm}

\begin{definition}[STM]\label{def:stm}
  \emph{Software transactional memory} (STM) is a concurrency control
  mechanism that provides atomic, consistent, and isolated transactions on
  shared memory, analogous to database transactions.
\end{definition}

STM is used in Haskell (\texttt{STM} monad), Clojure (\texttt{ref} and
\texttt{dosync}), and has been proposed for other languages.

\subsection{Distributed Computing Models}\label{sec:distributed-models}

Distributed programming paradigms include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Message passing}: MPI, Erlang/OTP---processes communicate
    by exchanging messages.
  \item \textbf{Shared nothing}: Each node has its own memory; communication
    is explicit.  Example: Google's MapReduce.
  \item \textbf{Shared memory (distributed)}: Distributed shared memory
    (DSM) provides a shared address space across nodes.
  \item \textbf{Data parallelism}: The same operation is applied to
    different partitions of data.  Example: CUDA, Spark.
  \item \textbf{Service-oriented}: Microservices communicate through
    well-defined APIs (REST, gRPC).
\end{itemize}

%% ============================================================
%% 14.10 How Paradigms Influence Compiler Design
%% ============================================================
\section{How Paradigms Influence Compiler Design}\label{sec:paradigm-compiler}

The choice of paradigm has profound implications for compiler and
interpreter design.

\subsection{Imperative Compilers}\label{sec:imperative-compilers}

Imperative language compilers focus on:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Register allocation}: Mapping variables to a limited number
    of hardware registers.
  \item \textbf{Instruction scheduling}: Reordering instructions to
    maximize pipeline utilization.
  \item \textbf{Alias analysis}: Determining when pointers may refer to the
    same memory location.
  \item \textbf{Loop optimization}: Unrolling, fusion, fission, vectorization.
\end{itemize}

These are the traditional concerns of compiler optimization
(\Cref{ch:finite-automata}ff.).

\subsection{Functional Compilers}\label{sec:functional-compilers}

Functional language compilers focus on:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Closure conversion}: Converting closures to pairs of code
    pointers and environment records.
  \item \textbf{Garbage collection}: Managing heap-allocated data with
    automatic reclamation.
  \item \textbf{Strictness analysis}: Determining which lazy computations
    can be made strict (evaluated eagerly).
  \item \textbf{Deforestation}: Eliminating intermediate data structures
    (e.g., fusing \texttt{map} and \texttt{filter}).
  \item \textbf{Type class resolution}: Translating type class dictionaries
    to explicit parameters.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Object-Oriented Compilers}\label{sec:oo-compilers}

Object-oriented language compilers focus on:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Method dispatch}: Implementing dynamic dispatch efficiently
    (inline caching, vtables, dispatch trees).
  \item \textbf{Object layout}: Arranging fields in memory for efficient
    access.
  \item \textbf{Class hierarchy analysis}: Using knowledge of the class
    hierarchy for optimization (devirtualization).
  \item \textbf{Garbage collection}: Managing object lifetimes.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Logic Language Compilers}\label{sec:logic-compilers}

Logic language compilers focus on:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Unification}: Implementing efficient unification (variable
    binding, trailing, copying).
  \item \textbf{Indexing}: Selecting clauses efficiently based on the first
    argument (first-argument indexing, multi-argument indexing).
  \item \textbf{Choice point management}: Efficient backtracking and
    garbage collection of choice points.
  \item \textbf{Tail recursion optimization}: Converting tail-recursive
    predicates to loops.
\end{itemize}

\begin{table}[htbp]
  \centering
  \caption{Paradigm-Specific Compiler Concerns}
  \label{tab:paradigm-compiler}
  \begin{tabular}{ll}
    \hline
    \textbf{Paradigm} & \textbf{Key Compiler Concerns} \\
    \hline
    Imperative & Register allocation, alias analysis, loop optimization \\
    Functional & Closure conversion, GC, strictness analysis, deforestation \\
    Object-oriented & Method dispatch, object layout, devirtualization \\
    Logic & Unification, clause indexing, backtracking \\
    Constraint & Constraint propagation, domain filtering, search \\
    Reactive & Change propagation, incremental recomputation \\
    Concurrent & Thread scheduling, synchronization, memory model \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{insight}
The paradigm determines the compiler's optimization strategy.  An imperative
compiler optimizes state transformations; a functional compiler optimizes
function compositions; an OO compiler optimizes message dispatches.
Understanding the paradigm is essential for building effective compilers.
\end{insight}

%% ============================================================
%% 14.11 Summary
%% ============================================================
\section{Summary}\label{sec:paradigms-summary}

Programming paradigms are not merely collections of features; they are
fundamentally different ways of thinking about computation.  Each paradigm
emphasizes certain aspects of programming (state transformation, function
composition, message passing, logical deduction, constraint satisfaction)
and provides abstractions suited to those aspects.

Modern languages increasingly blur the boundaries between paradigms,
incorporating features from multiple traditions.  This is a positive
development: it allows programmers to choose the most appropriate style for
each problem.  But it also increases the complexity of language
implementations, which must support the compilation strategies and
optimization techniques of multiple paradigms.

The relationship between paradigms and compilers is deep: the paradigm
determines what the compiler must optimize, what analyses it must perform,
and what runtime support it must provide.  A thorough understanding of
programming paradigms is therefore essential for compiler designers.
